# What gets published to smoald.com.
#
# Read by rsync in .github/workflows/deploy.yml:
#     rsync -a --delete --prune-empty-dirs --filter='merge .deployfilter' ./ .deploy/
#
# Rules are "+" (publish) and "-" (withhold), and the FIRST match wins, so
# order matters. This is an ALLOWLIST: the final "- *" withholds everything
# that no rule above has claimed.
#
# Why an allowlist rather than a list of exclusions:
#
# The deploy used to ship the repository root as-is, which published every
# working file on the site — notes, scripts, the workflow, the dev journal.
# One of those notes carried a third party's email address at a public URL.
# The first repair listed those paths as exclusions, and that works right up
# until somebody adds a folder nobody thought to exclude. Inverting it makes
# the default safe: a new folder is private until it is named here, and the
# failure mode is a missing page rather than a leaked one.
#
# Adding a real part of the site? Add a "+" line. The CI step after the
# deploy checks the internal paths really are 404, so a mistake in either
# direction fails the build rather than going quietly live.

# Never, under any circumstances.
- /.git/
- /.deploy/

# Let rsync walk into directories; --prune-empty-dirs drops the ones that
# end up with nothing in them. Without this, no rule below could ever match.
+ */

# The site itself.
+ /*.html
+ /ai/**
+ /assets/**
+ /legal/**

# Pages Functions — the enquiry form's server side.
+ /functions/**

# The prompt library, which is part of the portfolio.
+ /prompts/**

# Root-level files the site or search engines need.
+ /_redirects
+ /robots.txt
+ /sitemap.xml
+ /LICENSE
+ /README.md

# Everything else stays private.
- *
